# Target C-II — FREE ancestry couples external population to one outer-face near-unit run

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only strengthening of two-sided FREE absorption  
**Scope:** non-anchored FREE reflection modules, all-inactive two-sided activation ancestry  
**Dependencies:** two-sided gate absorption; two-boundary slack tradeoff; two-sided outer-face localization; C65 `F=conv B`  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_external_population_run_tradeoff_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let

\[
Z_{\rm ext}:=|A\setminus F|,
\qquad F=\operatorname{conv}B,
\]

where `B` is the complete actual-diameter endpoint set.  The preceding outer-face
localization proves that every inactive upper-gate child lies outside `F`.  Since an
activation ancestry never activates one source vertex twice, the number `U` of upper
gates on one ancestry satisfies the exact source-level bound

\[
\boxed{U\le Z_{\rm ext}.}
\tag{ER.1}
\]

This turns the tunable upper-poor theorem into a direct external-population/run
tradeoff.  Let `S_*` be the largest support-depth span of a maximal consecutive
lower-gate run on an all-inactive ancestry reaching the near-opposite threshold.  Then

\[
\boxed{
(Z_{\rm ext}+1)S_*
\ge D-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{ER.2}
\]

Every such lower run lies in the original outer contact face, and every maximal lower
run starts at a source point outside `F`.

The longest run also has a quantitative near-unit matching.  If it has `R_*` edges,
then

\[
R_*\ge S_*-t_D,
\qquad
t_D<h_*+D^{-1/3},
\qquad
h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52},
\tag{ER.3}
\]

and it contains a vertex-disjoint matching of at least

\[
\boxed{{R_*\over4}-O(1)}
\tag{ER.4}
\]

old fully inactive outer-face pairs satisfying

\[
\boxed{
1<|x-y|
\le1+{2t_D\over R_*}.
}
\tag{ER.5}
\]

Thus whenever `S_*->infinity`, the selected outer-face run contains
`(1/4-o(1))S_*` disjoint pairs of length

\[
1+O(1/S_*).
\]

In exponent form, for every fixed `0<=alpha<1`,

\[
Z_{\rm ext}=O(D^\alpha)
\]

forces one outer-face run with

\[
\boxed{S_*=\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})}
\tag{ER.6}
\]

and a matching of the same order whose excess above one is

\[
\boxed{O(D^{\alpha-1}).}
\tag{ER.7}
\]

For example, `Z_ext=O(D^(1/4))` forces the reflection-matched
`D^(3/4)` run without making a separate upper-gate count assumption.

Equivalently, absence of a support-deep outer-face run forces external population:
for every positive threshold `S_D`, if every maximal lower run has support span below
`S_D`, then

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm ext}
\ge {D-O(\sqrt D)\over S_D}-1.
}
\tag{ER.8}

This is a direct bridge between the reflection repair and the canonical external-cap
population variable of Sections 77--80.

---

## 1. Every upper gate spends one distinct external source vertex

Fix an all-inactive ancestry

\[
z_0,z_1,\ldots,z_m.
\]

The activation process assigns strictly increasing activation times along an ancestry.
Every child is fixed before its first activation and remains active afterwards, so a
source vertex cannot occur twice as an activated child on the same ancestry.

By the two-sided outer-face localization theorem, if `z_j z_{j+1}` is an upper gate,
then

\[
z_{j+1}\notin F.
\]

Different upper gates therefore have different children in `A\setminus F`, proving
(ER.1).

The same observation also identifies the starting vertex of every maximal lower run.
The first run begins at the FREE blocker root, which lies outside `F`; every later run
begins immediately after an upper gate, at its child, which also lies outside `F`.
Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{every maximal lower run starts outside }F
\text{ and then travels entirely in the old outer contact face.}
}
\tag{ER.9}

It may enter one or more convex outer pockets inside `F`; ER.9 does not assert that the
whole run remains outside the force hull.

---

## 2. Total lower support progress is almost `D`

Write

\[
A:=a_{z_m}-a_{z_0}.
\]

The near-opposite activation theorem gives

\[
A>D-O(\sqrt D).
\tag{ER.10}
\]

For an ancestry edge let

\[
\Delta_j=s_{j+1}-s_j>0,
\]

so

\[
\sum_j\Delta_j=s_m\le t_D.
\tag{ER.11}
\]

Let `g_j=a_{z_{j+1}}-a_{z_j}` be its signed support-depth increment.  For a lower gate,
`g_j>0`; for an upper gate the first-hit sign calculation gives

\[
g_j<\Delta_j.
\tag{ER.12}
\]

Put

\[
G_{\rm low}:=\sum_{j\in E_{\rm low}}g_j,
\qquad
G_{\rm up}:=\sum_{j\in E_{\rm up}}g_j.
\]

Then

\[
A=G_{\rm low}+G_{\rm up},
\]

and by (ER.11)--(ER.12),

\[
G_{\rm up}<\sum_{j\in E_{\rm up}}\Delta_j\le t_D.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
G_{\rm low}>A-t_D=D-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{ER.13}

Upper-gate backtracking can be arbitrarily large and only makes `G_low` larger.  No
sign assumption on the total upper progress is needed.

---

## 3. External population bounds the number of lower runs

Deleting the `U` upper edges partitions the lower edges into at most `U+1` maximal
consecutive lower runs.  The support span of a lower run is exactly the sum of its
positive `g_j` values.  If `S_*` is the largest such span, then

\[
G_{\rm low}\le(U+1)S_*.
\tag{ER.14}

Combining (ER.1), (ER.13), and (ER.14),

\[
(Z_{\rm ext}+1)S_*
\ge(U+1)S_*
\ge G_{\rm low}
>D-O(\sqrt D),
\]

which proves ER.2.

The contrapositive ER.8 is immediate.

---

## 4. The selected run inherits the global `O(1)` lower-slack ledger

Take a maximal lower run attaining `S_*`, with `R_*` edges.  For a lower gate,

\[
1<|z_j-z_{j+1}|=1+\sigma_j,
\qquad
\sigma_j\le\Delta_j,
\]

and its support gain satisfies

\[
0<g_j\le |z_j-z_{j+1}|=1+\sigma_j.
\tag{ER.15}

The global activation-time telescope gives

\[
\sum_{j\in E_{\rm low}}\sigma_j\le t_D.
\tag{ER.16}

Hence on the selected run

\[
S_*
=\sum_{j\in R_*}g_j
\le R_*+\sum_{j\in R_*}\sigma_j
\le R_*+t_D,
\]

which is ER.3.

By Markov counting, fewer than `R_*/2` run edges can satisfy

\[
\sigma_j>{2t_D\over R_*}.
\]

At least `R_*/2` edges are therefore good.  Split the path edges by index parity;
one parity contains at least half the good edges and is a matching.  This yields at
least `R_*/4-O(1)` vertex-disjoint good edges and proves ER.4--ER.5.

The two-sided outer-face theorem puts every run vertex in the original outer contact
face, so this matching is outer-face localized.

---

## 5. Exponent consequences

If

\[
Z_{\rm ext}\le C D^\alpha
\]

for a fixed `C` and `0<=alpha<1`, ER.2 gives

\[
S_*
\ge {D-O(\sqrt D)\over CD^\alpha+1}
=\Omega(D^{1-\alpha}).
\]

Then ER.3--ER.5 give a matching of order `D^(1-alpha)` and excess

\[
O(D^{\alpha-1}).
\]

At `alpha=1/4`, this is exactly the geometric scale of the current slow reflection
braid:

\[
S_*=\Omega(D^{3/4}),
\qquad
|M_*|=\Omega(D^{3/4}),
\qquad
|e|-1=O(D^{-3/4}).
\]

At `alpha=0`, bounded external population forces a linear outer-face run with
inverse-`D` near-unit matching.

The theorem is deliberately stated as a tradeoff rather than a closure: canonical
Section 80 allows `Z_ext=o(D^2)` in the no-macroscopic-container branch, which by
itself is too weak to make ER.2 macroscopic.  What is new is that every interruption
of the FREE lower corridor has an exact source-level price in the already canonical
external population variable.

**Disposition:** proved `U<=Z_ext` and the hyperbolic external-population / outer-face-run
tradeoff ER.2, with an inherited near-unit matching ER.4--ER.5.  C-II and Level I
remain open.
